# Canonical gate-check vocabulary + category split for phased-execution.
# SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH (F5 pattern, like sizing.env): scripts/phase-graph.sh
# sources this file and viewer/server/analysis/gates.ts reads the same file, so
# the bash engine and the console can never disagree about which Gate-check
# types exist, which category a gate falls into, or what an undeclared gate
# reads as. Change them here only.
#
# Plain KEY="VALUE" (sourced by bash 3.2) — space-separated type tokens.
#
# Categories (what --gate-kind answers):
#   human — a person must act. `manual` gates, and a directive whose type is
#           not on the list (fail-safe — and a lint failure, F24). Cleared only
#           by an approval: the console's Gate card or scripts/gate-approve.sh.
#   ai    — an AI session may verify/do/clear it (a person may still approve).
#           The boot prompt makes the gate check the session's first task.
#   auto  — the engine evaluates it by itself (dates, phase completion, a
#           read-only command). Nothing to approve unless overriding.
#
# GATE_DEFAULT is the TYPE a *(GATED)* heading with no Gate-check line reads
# as. It was `human` until 5.0.0; the sep-review audit (ZTD-5) found 84 of 143
# gated headings in the estate demanding a person, 15 of them by that accident
# alone, against documents that say `ai` is the bias. The default now says so —
# and --lint still fails the heading (F24 `gate-directive-missing`), because a
# default that answers for the board is not the author having said it.
GATE_TYPES="phase phases plan cmd date deadline by manual ai"
GATE_TYPES_HUMAN="manual"
GATE_TYPES_AI="ai"
GATE_DEFAULT="ai"
